Wow, a change like that to satisfy the speedrunners fraction?!

(Jumping into the thread quite late I really wondered about that
decision. Changing Nethack towards a jump-and-run game? *shudder*)

Even if it's not only the speedrunners but also the "Nethack Is Too
Difficult, Help Me!" folks, or the "In This Specific Game The RNG
Has Not Created Enough Foos And No Bars, I Am Doomed!" people. Wow!

From my experience those games (games lacking something important)
are amongst the most interesting ones, because one has to *think*
and _work on alternatives_. Getting everything that I like in any
amount I need or even guaranteed is taking away an essential part
of that game. You may need to travel forth and back, look at places
you intended to skip, take risky challenges to enhance your chance
to get it; the game gets less straightforward, and more interesting.
YMMV.

(Go to A and get X, then go to B and get Y, then go to C and get Z,
is not that interesting as a game design concept. In Nethack this is
already the case to a certain degree; to change it further in that
direction is not desirable. In my book.)

("Where is my *guaranteed* bag of holding? Or even a guaranteed sack
before dungeon level 5? Where is my guaranteed MR or reflection? Why
is that not added? Help!")

In addition; you don't need candles until the very late game. There
is a lot time to find some spare even in Gehennom. In all the games
I played (reached the VS ~140 times in the last decade) I needed to
wish for candles only once; and for that specific game I cannot even
say that I've thoroughly explored the Gehennom dungeons or activated
all options (like polypiling).

